Z||000003||recluse||08-27-2001||04:29 PM||||The stainless brake lines from the Fiero store are $99.95<P>Did you use a line wrench on the brake lines??  A regular wrench will strip the nut part very easily.  Spray some WD-40 or something similar on them too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DreXteR||08-27-2001||07:15 PM||||I use everithing to take it off, but wasnt successfull. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||08-27-2001||08:21 PM||||when I cant get the brake lines loose with a line wrench I use what looks like channel locks (made by the same company) but the grabbing end is somewhat rounded and the harder you pull the tighter it grips-these have never failed me.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||jelly2m8||08-27-2001||09:33 PM||||Get 2 pairs of FLAT jawed visegrips, the only thing the curved jawed ones are good for is really making a mess of nuts and bolts...<BR> Use 1 pair of flat jaw visegrips on the hex of the flare nut, you will leave a few marks on the flare nut, but nothing to worry about. Take the second pair of visegrips and put them on the Brass/ Steel end of the old flex hose. Now turn the flare nut enough just to break it loose from the flex hose. Use the vise grips on the flex hose to hold everything steady, so you dont rip the mount out of the frame. <BR> Once the flare nut is broke free , try backing the nut out of the hose, if the steel line turns with the flare nut, stop!.<BR> What you do is remove the clip that holds the hose in the mount, slide the hose with the steel line still attached to it, down through the bracket. Then turn the hose off of the steel line/flarenut. Now it will be easier to get that flare nut spinning freely on the steel line, soak it with penetrating fluid, and work it back and forth untill it makes a complete turn or 2. I like using a little bit of heat, but it makes a quick fire , so If you do that, have a little bit of water handy to splash on it.<P> Then you will be ready to install your new flex hoses on the original steel lines.<P> <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-27-2001||10:28 PM||||If the lines are stuck that bad, take a look over the rest of the brakeline,  It might be worth changing them all (You dont want the weakest place to fail)..<P>I bought the line in bulk for mine and got a iso flare tool loaner and replaced everything..<BR> Well worth it.<P><BR>If the lines are in good shape...just stuck..  jelly2m8 is correct, Vicegrips will do it.. just be careful..<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DreXteR||08-27-2001||11:59 PM||||I try already with the vise grips, didnt make the trick. I try to losse it so hard that the lines strat to turn instead of the nut. Also the area for use the tool from the nut(like the first post) is round. Trying to take it off the nut I damage the nut and didnt move a mm.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I guess that buy it is the best bet, anyway is a 16 yrs old car.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000009||Primaris||08-28-2001||12:28 AM||||I just learned this first hand.  You must use a 12mm LINE wrench and a 17mm or 3/4in (I think it’s 3/4in for stock and 17mm for Earls lines).  Use the 12mm line wrench on the metal brake line and the other on the flexible part.  If you do not use 2 wrenches you will not get the lines to separate.  If you striped the fitting I would use vice grips in place of the 12mm.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
